        <div id="how-ro-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Reverse Osmosis Works, and What It Removes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A reverse osmosis membrane is a sheet of thin-film composite wound into a cartridge, with pores so small that water molecules pass and most dissolved salts, metals and larger organic molecules do not. Household water pressure pushes water across the membrane. Clean water, called permeate, comes out one side; the rejected minerals and contaminants leave in a continuous stream of concentrate that goes to the drain. That drain stream is not a defect. It is how the membrane keeps itself from clogging.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because the membrane works on dissolved solids rather than adsorption, RO handles contaminants that carbon alone struggles with: nitrate, fluoride, arsenic, hexavalent chromium, many PFAS compounds and total dissolved solids generally. The carbon pre-filters in front of the membrane take out chlorine, which would otherwise attack the thin-film membrane, and the carbon post-filter catches any taste or odor that slips through. Sediment pre-filters protect everything downstream from grit.
          </p>

          <p>
            What RO does not do is make unsafe water safe. Manufacturers say it plainly in their manuals: these systems are for use on water that is already microbiologically safe, such as municipal water or a tested well. A UV stage, like the one on the G3P800, adds a layer of protection, but it is not a substitute for proper well treatment. If you are on a private well with bacteria or high iron, fix that first at the point of entry and use RO as the drinking-water polish.
          </p>

          <p>
            The single most useful thing you can do before buying is to know what is in your water. Municipal customers can read their utility's annual Consumer Confidence Report. Well owners should send a sample to a certified lab. If the report shows high nitrate, arsenic, fluoride you want gone, or PFAS, RO is the right tool. If it shows only chlorine taste, a carbon filter is cheaper, wastes no water, and will make you just as happy.
          </p>

