          <p>
            Two patterns jump out. First, the tankless systems are dramatically more efficient. A pumped tankless unit runs the membrane at a consistent high pressure, which is what lets Waterdrop publish 2:1 and 3:1 ratios. Conventional tank systems run on line pressure that drops as the tank fills, and the back-pressure from the tank makes the membrane less efficient as it goes; that is why their makers tend not to publish a ratio at all, and why owners commonly report three to four gallons of concentrate per gallon of permeate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, the tank systems win on simplicity and running cost. Nothing to plug in, nothing to fail electrically, and three of them use standard-size cartridges you can buy from any filter brand. If your sink cabinet has the room and your house has 50 psi or more, that is a perfectly good way to get RO water, and it is still how most RO systems in American kitchens work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat the certification column as the most important one. NSF/ANSI 58 is the standard written specifically for reverse osmosis drinking water systems and covers structural integrity, materials safety and TDS reduction, plus any extra contaminant claims the maker chooses to certify. A system that claims to remove PFAS or lead should have those claims listed on its performance data sheet, which you can request from the manufacturer or find on the certifier's website.
          </p>
