        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best reverse osmosis system for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes with an outlet under the kitchen sink, the Waterdrop G3P800 is the best reverse osmosis system: 800 GPD, a 3:1 pure-to-drain ratio, NSF/ANSI 58 certification and a TDS faucet. Without an outlet, the iSpring RCC7AK is the best tank system. Renters should look at the AquaTru Classic countertop unit, which needs no plumbing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is tankless reverse osmosis better than a tank system?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most kitchens, yes. Tankless systems make water on demand, so flow never tapers, they waste far less water thanks to a built-in pump, and they free up cabinet space. The trade-offs are that they need a 120V outlet under the sink and use proprietary filters that cost more. Tank systems remain the better choice where power is unavailable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much water does a reverse osmosis system waste?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the design. High-efficiency tankless systems publish ratios of 2:1 or 3:1, meaning one gallon of concentrate per two or three gallons of drinking water. Conventional tank systems running on line pressure commonly waste three to four gallons per gallon made. A permeate pump or higher feed pressure improves a tank system's ratio.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does reverse osmosis remove fluoride and PFAS?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Reverse osmosis is one of the few home technologies that reliably reduces fluoride, and certified systems also reduce many PFAS compounds, including PFOA and PFOS. Check the system's performance data sheet for the specific claim, because certification covers only the contaminants listed. The AquaTru Classic, for example, lists P473 certification for PFOA and PFOS reduction.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is reverse osmosis water bad for you because it removes minerals?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Reverse osmosis water is safe to drink, and the minerals your body needs come overwhelmingly from food rather than water. RO water can taste flat, which is why many systems add a remineralization stage. Choose one because you prefer the taste, not because RO water is unhealthy without it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do you change reverse osmosis filters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pre-filters usually last six to twelve months, post-filters and mineral cartridges about a year, and the membrane two to four years, depending on water hardness and chlorine levels. The most reliable signal for the membrane is a TDS reading: when the RO water's TDS rises noticeably relative to the tap, the membrane is due.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a reverse osmosis system myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, most people can install an under-sink RO system in two to three hours with basic tools. You connect a feed valve to the cold line, fit a drain saddle, mount the faucet and run tubing. Drilling a faucet hole through stone or quartz is the step most people hand to a pro. Countertop units like the AquaTru need no installation at all.
              </p>
            </div>

